⚠ AND THE LEGACY READING IS VOID RATHER THAN NEGATIVE, which had to be caught
before the flip or it would have been read as an A/B. Under legacy, showBody's
own argument is auto-unwrapped on the way in, so showBody receives the CALLER's
node and prints a matching body no matter what the wrapper holds. THE WRAPPER IS
UNOBSERVABLE FROM INCANT WHILE THE UNWRAP STANDS. This fixture can therefore
CONFIRM the flip; it cannot discriminate against legacy from inside the language.
That is not a defect in the fixture -- it is the two-half law restated as an
epistemic fact.

THE MECHANISM, probed at the bind site rather than inferred:

    BEFORE  ruleArg tag=argument   isArg=1  body=0x..2eb0
    AFTER   ruleArg tag=asSubject  isArg=0  body=0x..0230

The adoption WORKS. That is the problem. `GroupBody` carries the node's IDENTITY
-- tag, isArgument, isLocal, noPrint -- in the same struct as its STORAGE --
data, gGroup, groupList, gCount/gText. Adopting the caller's body therefore
adopts the caller's NAME as well, so the argument slot stops being called
`argument` and stops being flagged as one. The name `argument` inside the body
then resolves to nothing and mints a fresh action LOCAL (bear-trap #39), which
is the stray node with the old body in the trace above.

ONE CHANNEL, ONE MEANING, one level down from where the campaign has been
reading it: not two flags on a node, but IDENTITY AND STORAGE IN ONE STRUCT.
Bind-by-body wants the storage half and must not take the identity half.

